e obtain the complete\ncharacterisation stated above.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); otherwise it\nis called **non‑sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nAssume that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covers all points of\n\\(T_n\\).  Let  \n\n\\[\nk=|\\{L\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L\\text{ is sunny}\\}|\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\ns=n-k\n\\]\n\nbe the numbers of sunny and non‑sunny lines, respectively.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  Two forced non‑sunny lines\n\nThe column \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points \\((1,b)\\;(1\\le b\\le n)\\).\nIf \\(\\mathcal L\\) contained no vertical line, each line would meet this column\nin at most one point, so at least \\(n\\) distinct lines would be needed – a\ncontradiction.  Hence \\(x=1\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal L\\); it is non‑sunny.\n\nThe same argument applied to the row \\(y=1\\) shows that \\(y=1\\) also belongs to\n\\(\\mathcal L\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\ns\\ge 2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The interior set\n\nDelete the two lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\).  The points that remain are\n\n\\[\nT'_n=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\},\n\\qquad |T'_n|=\\frac{(n-1)(n-2)}2 .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll points of \\(T'_n\\) have to be covered by the remaining\n\\(s-2=n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines together with the \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.3  How many points can a sunny line contain?\n\nLet \\(L\\) be a sunny line.  Its slope is different from \\(-1\\); therefore the\nintersection of \\(L\\) with the family of lines \\(x+y=c\\) (the “anti‑diagonals”)\ncontains at most one point.  Inside the triangle \\(T'_n\\) the anti‑diagonals\nhave lengths \\(1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).  Hence a\nsunny line can meet \\(T'_n\\) in at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints.  This bound is attained precisely by the lines of slope \\(1\\),\ni.e. by the lines \\(x-y=c\\).\n\nThus\n\n\\[\n\\text{each sunny line covers }\\le M:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\text{ points of }T'_n .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.4  Counting points\n\nThe \\(k\\) sunny lines together cover at most \\(kM\\) points of \\(T'_n\\).\nThe remaining \\(|T'_n|-kM\\) points must be covered by the\n\\(n-2-k\\) non‑sunny lines that are still available.  Any non‑sunny line can\nintersect \\(T'_n\\) in at most \\(M\\) points (the same bound (3) holds for a\nvertical, horizontal or anti‑diagonal line, because the triangle \\(T'_n\\) is\ncontained in a strip of width \\(M\\) measured along each of the three forbidden\ndirections).  Hence\n\n\\[\n|T'_n|-kM\\le (n-2-k)M .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(|T'_n|=\\dfrac{(n-1)(n-2)}2\\) and simplifying gives\n\n\\[\nk\\le M=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore any admissible configuration satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\) in the range\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Non‑sunny horizontal lines\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose second\ncoordinate satisfies \\(b\\le n-k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The uncovered part\n\nA point of \\(T_n\\) not covered by (5) must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nb\\ge n-k+1,\\qquad a\\le n+1-b\\le k .\n\\]\n\nHence the uncovered points form the set  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k+1-a\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is exactly the triangular set \\(T_k\\) defined in (3) with size \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Sunny covering of \\(T_k\\)\n\nFor each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\;x-y=i-1\\qquad\\bigl(\\text{equivalently }y=x-(i-1)\\bigr).\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(1\\); therefore every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\nInside the triangle \\(T_k\\) the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains precisely the points\n\\((i,b)\\) with \\(1\\le b\\le k+1-i\\); i.e. it covers the whole \\(i\\)-th column of\n\\(T_k\\).  Consequently the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers all of\n\\(T_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.4  The whole family\n\nThe union of the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines (5) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (6) is a\nset of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By construction it contains precisely\n\\(k\\) sunny lines and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nthere exists a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe upper bound (4) and the explicit construction of §2 together give a complete\ncharacterisation:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The admissible values of }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor } .\n\\]\n\nNo other non‑negative integer can occur.  ∎"
