\mathcal L_n\\) covers \\(T_n\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Counting sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n^{\\!*}= \\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge 2\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe map \\((a,b)\\mapsto \\dfrac{b}{a}\\) is injective on \\(T_n^{\\!*}\\) (the argument above).  \nConsequently the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) equals \\(|T_n^{\\!*}|\\).\n\nSince \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) have \\(b=1\\),\n\\[\n|T_n^{\\!*}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) contains \\(\\dfrac{n(n-1)}2\\) sunny lines and one non‑sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nWe now show that for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n .\n\\]\n\nAll are non‑sunny, they are distinct, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\);\nhence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(y=b\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nSelect any \\(n\\) distinct points of \\(T_n^{\\!*}\\) (for instance\n\\((1,2),(2,3),\\dots ,(n,n+1)\\)).  \nFor each chosen point \\((a_i,b_i)\\) take the sunny line \\(\\ell_i : y=\\dfrac{b_i}{a_i}x\\).\nAll \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct and sunny, and by construction each point of \\(T_n^{\\!*}\\) lies on the line that corresponds to it.\nThe remaining points of \\(T_n\\) (those with \\(b=1\\)) are covered because each such point\n\\((a,1)\\) also belongs to the line \\(\\ell_{a,1}\\) with slope \\(\\frac{1}{a}\\); this line is among the chosen \\(n\\) lines because we may replace the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) by the sunny line through the origin and \\((a,1)\\).  Hence all points are covered and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.3  General \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n^{\\!*}\\mid 1\\le a\\le k\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(S_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) points, because for each \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) the\nlargest admissible \\(b\\) is \\(n+1-a\\ge 2\\).  \nFor each \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) take the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}: y=\\dfrac{b}{a}x\\).\nThese \\(k\\) lines are distinct (injectivity of the slope map) and are sunny.\n\nNow we need \\(n-k\\) further lines to reach a total of \\(n\\).  \nUse the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;y=n-k .\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny and distinct.  \n\n*Coverage.*  \n– Points with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lie on the corresponding horizontal line.  \n– Points with \\(b\\ge n-k+1\\) satisfy \\(b\\ge 2\\) (because \\(n\\ge 3\\) and \\(k\\le n-1\\)).\n   Write such a point as \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\le k\\) (otherwise \\(a\\ge k+1\\) would give\n   \\(a+b\\ge (k+1)+(n-k+1)=n+2\\), contradicting the defining inequality).\n   Hence \\((a,b)\\in S_k\\) and therefore lies on the sunny line \\(\\ell_{a,b}\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered, exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny, and the total\nnumber of lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), an explicit construction of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(T_n\\), and  \n* contain precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
