ers all points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the old points are covered by \\(\\mathcal S_n\\), the new ones by \\(\\ell_{n+1}\\)).  ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Mixed sunny / non‑sunny coverings (Lemma 2)\n\n> **Lemma 2.**  \n> Let \\(n\\ge 3\\).  For every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines such that  \n> (i) every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\);  \n> (ii) exactly \\(k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) are sunny.\n\n*Proof by induction on \\(n\\).*\n\n*Base \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe four possibilities are exhibited below; each family contains three distinct lines and the required number of sunny lines.\n\n| \\(k\\) | lines (ordered) |\n|------|-----------------|\n| 0 | \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;x=1\\) (all non‑sunny) |\n| 1 | \\(y=x,\\;y=2,\\;x=2\\) (only \\(y=x\\) sunny) |\n| 2 | \\(y=x,\\;y=2x,\\;x=2\\) (first two sunny) |\n| 3 | the three sunny lines of Lemma 1 for \\(n=3\\). |\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume the statement true for some \\(n\\ge 3\\); we prove it for \\(n+1\\).\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n+1\\).\n\n*Case 1: \\(k\\le n\\).*  \nTake a configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) for the triangle \\(T_n\\) (which exists by the induction hypothesis).  \nAdd the horizontal line  \n\n\\[\nh_{n+1}:\\; y=n+1 .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nThe line \\(h_{n+1}\\) is non‑sunny, it is distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) (its \\(y\\)-intercept is larger than any intercept occurring in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\)), and it covers precisely the new points of \\(T_{n+1}\\) whose second coordinate equals \\(n+1\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}= \\mathcal L_{n,k}\\cup\\{h_{n+1}\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\((n+1)\\) distinct lines, still exactly \\(k\\) of them sunny, and it covers every point of \\(T_{n+1}\\) (the old points are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), the new ones by \\(h_{n+1}\\)).\n\n*Case 2: \\(k=n+1\\).*  \nHere we need all lines to be sunny.  By Lemma 1 we have an all‑sunny family \\(\\mathcal S_{\\,n+1}\\) of \\(n+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{n+1}\\).  Set \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,n+1}:=\\mathcal S_{\\,n+1}\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\) a suitable family \\(\\mathcal L_{n+1,k}\\) exists.  This completes the induction. ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\nFrom Lemma 2 (applied with the given \\(n\\)) we obtain, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a collection of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements of the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTogether with the obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) established in (2.1), we have proved that the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n∎"
