mbination of \\((c,1)\\) and \\((1,c+1)\\); consequently it also lies on \\(s_{j,c}\\).  \n   Hence replacing \\(\\ell_j\\) by \\(s_{j,c}\\) leaves the covering property intact while increasing the number of sunny lines by one.\n\n   Repeating this replacement for any \\(k\\) of the \\(n\\) lines yields a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\).\n\n4. **All\u2011sunny configuration (Lemma\u202f3).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) sunny lines\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_i:\\; y = \\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i+1},\n   \\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n   \\tag{3.1}\n   \\]\n   As in Lemma\u202f2 each \\(\\sigma_i\\) contains the two points \\((i,1)\\) and \\((1,i+1)\\); both belong to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Moreover every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) can be expressed as a convex combination of a suitable pair \\((i,1),(1,i+1)\\) with \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the corresponding line \\(\\sigma_i\\).  \n   Thus the family \\(\\{\\sigma_i\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) covers \\(T_n\\) and all its members are sunny, giving the case \\(k=n\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   Lemma\u202f1 provides the case \\(k=0\\); Lemma\u202f2 yields all intermediate values up to \\(k=n-2\\); Lemma\u202f3 supplies the extreme value \\(k=n\\).  \n   Therefore for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines contains  \n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   The case \\(k=n-1\\) remains unresolved."

