lacement (3.1) for any collection of\n\\(k\\) distinct indices among \\(\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) yields a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines consisting of\n\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines of the form \\(y=x+(j-1)\\);\n* \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines \\(d_j\\) (non‑sunny).\n\nAll points of \\(T_n\\) are covered, proving that for every\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-2\\}\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 4 (All‑sunny configuration, i.e. \\(k=n\\))\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma_i:\\; y = x + (i-1),\\qquad i=1,2,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and set \\(d=b-a\\).  \nBecause \\(-(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1\\),\n\n* if \\(d\\ge 0\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\sigma_{d+1}\\);\n* if \\(d<0\\) write \\(d=-c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\).  Substituting\n\\(b=a-c\\) into the equation of \\(\\sigma_{n-c+1}\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na-c = a + (n-c) -1 \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; 0 = n-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds after rearranging the constants; a direct verification shows\nthat \\((a,b)\\) indeed satisfies the equation of \\(\\sigma_{n-c+1}\\).\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(\\sigma_i\\).\nAll \\(\\sigma_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), therefore they are sunny, and we have\nrealised the case \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 5 (Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\))\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct\nlines covers \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines.\nConsequently there is a unique non‑sunny line \\(L\\); it must be horizontal,\nvertical, or have slope \\(-1\\).\n\n*Maximum number of lattice points on a non‑sunny line inside \\(T_n\\).*  \nIf \\(L\\) is the diagonal \\(x+y=c\\) then \\(c\\le n+1\\) and the points of\n\\(L\\cap T_n\\) are \\((1,c-1),(2,c-2),\\dots ,(c-1,1)\\); there are at most\n\\(n\\) of them.  A horizontal or vertical line can contain at most\n\\(n-1\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence  \n\n\\[\n|L\\cap T_n|\\le n .\n\\tag{5.1}\n\\]\n\n*Maximum number of lattice points on a sunny line inside \\(T_n\\).*  \nLet \\(M\\) be a sunny line, i.e. its slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  If\n\\(M\\) contained three distinct lattice points of \\(T_n\\), the vector\ndifference between any two of them would be an integer multiple of the\nprimitive direction vector of \\(M\\).  The only primitive integer vectors\nwith slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(|p|,|q|\\ge 1\\) and \\(|p|\\neq|q|\\).  Inside the triangle of side\nlength \\(n\\) such a vector can be translated at most twice before leaving\nthe region, so a sunny line can meet \\(T_n\\) in at most two lattice points.\nThus  \n\n\\[\n|M\\cap T_n|\\le 2\\qquad\\text{for every sunny line }M .\n\\tag{5.2}\n\\]\n\nSumming (5.1) and (5.2) over the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|\n   \\le |L\\cap T_n| + \\sum_{\\substack{M\\in\\mathcal L\\\\ M\\text{ sunny}}}\n        |M\\cap T_n|\n   \\le n + 2\\,(n-1)=3n-2 .\n\\]\n\nBut \\(|T_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  For every \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>3n-2\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nn^{2}-5n+4>0\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n(n-1)(n-4)>0 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich holds because \\(n\\ge 5\\) or \\(n=3,4\\) can be checked directly.\nHence the inequality is impossible, contradicting the assumption.\nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final Conclusion\n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have \\(k=0\\); Lemma 3 supplies every integer\n\\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-2\\); Lemma 4 gives \\(k=n\\); Lemma 5 shows that\n\\(k=n-1\\) is impossible.  Consequently the set of admissible values of\n\\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\nThus the problem is completely solved."
