\n   points at most, whereas \\(|S_n|=n(n+1)/2\\) points have to be covered.\n   For even \\(n\\) we have \\(\\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\); hence\n   \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.\n\n4. **Construction for all admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Common part.*  \n   For each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define the sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y=x+(i-1) .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Its slope is \\(1\\) (sunny).  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (a,\\;a+i-1),\\qquad\n   1\\le a\\le \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Construction for a given \\(k\\).*  \n\n   - Take the first \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n   - Remove from \\(S_n\\) all points already covered by these \\(k\\) lines.\n     The remaining points have pairwise distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates; indeed,\n     for a fixed \\(x=a\\) the possible \\(y\\)-values are \\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\),\n     and the equations \\(y=a+i-1\\) (with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\)) have already taken\n     at most one of them.  \n   - For each still uncovered \\(x\\)-coordinate \\(a\\) draw the vertical line\n     \\(x=a\\).  These vertical lines are non\u2011sunny and are all distinct.\n     The number of such vertical lines equals the number of uncovered\n     \\(x\\)-coordinates, which is exactly \\(n-k\\).\n\n   Consequently we obtain a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\n   lines that together cover every point of \\(S_n\\).  All lines are distinct,\n   and the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n5. **Adjustment for even \\(n\\).**  \n   When \\(n\\) is even, the construction above with \\(k=n\\) would require\n   \\(0\\) vertical lines.  In this case the union of the \\(n\\) lines\n   \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) covers only  \n\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^{n}\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   =\\frac{n^{2}}{2}<\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\); the missing points are precisely those with\n   \\(x+y=n+2\\).  Hence for even \\(n\\) the construction works for every\n   \\(k\\le n-1\\) (the missing points are then covered by the required\n   vertical line \\(x=n+1\\)).  Together with the impossibility proved in\n   step\u202f3 this shows that for even \\(n\\) the largest possible number of\n   sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\nCombining the constructions with the impossibility result yields the\ncomplete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \u201c\\(a+b\\le n+1\\)\u201d describes a right\u2011angled isosceles triangle\nwith vertices \\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).  \nA simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A bound for a sunny line  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis,\nthe \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite a sunny line in the form  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:\\; \\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;\n\\alpha\\beta\\neq0,\\;\\alpha+\\beta\\neq0,\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)=1\\).\n\nLet \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_t,b_t)\\) be the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on\n\\(\\ell\\), ordered so that \\(a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_t\\).  \nBecause \\(\\beta\\neq0\\) the line is not vertical, hence the\n\\(b_i\\)\u2019s are strictly decreasing:\n\n\\[\nb_1>b_2>\\dots>b_t .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nFor consecutive points set  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta a_i=a_{i+1}-a_i>0,\\qquad\n\\Delta b_i=b_{i+1}-b_i<0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince both \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_{i+1},b_{i+1})\\) satisfy (7),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\Delta a_i+\\beta\\Delta b_i=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{\\Delta b_i}{\\Delta a_i}=-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are non\u2011zero integers of opposite\nsign, whence  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sum \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\).  From (9),\n\n\\[\ns_{i+1}-s_i=\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause the signs of \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are opposite,\n\\(|\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i|\\le |\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|-2\\).  Using (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|s_{i+1}-s_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHence the values \\(s_i\\) taken on the line differ by at least \\(2\\).\nFor points of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\); the interval\n\\([2,n+1]\\) contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nintegers that are pairwise at distance \\(\\ge2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma\u202f1 each line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points,\nso the total number of covered points does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}{2},   & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\dfrac{n^{2}}{2}<\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\\) by (6);\nhence the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered.  Therefore for even\n\\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A family of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1) .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\); thus \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) iff \\(b=a+i-1\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(2a\\le n+2-i\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap S_n=\\bigl\\{(a,\\;a+i-1)\\mid\n1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nand \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 \u2013 sunny lines.*  \nTake the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Step 2 \u2013 points still uncovered.*  \nRemove from \\(S_n\\) all points belonging to the chosen sunny lines.\nFor a fixed abscissa \\(x=a\\) the possible ordinates are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\).  \nAmong these, at most one equals \\(a+i-1\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\); therefore\nat most one point with abscissa \\(a\\) has already been covered.\nConsequently each uncovered abscissa appears **exactly once** among the\nremaining points.\n\n*Step 3 \u2013 vertical lines.*  \nFor every uncovered abscissa \\(a\\) draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny, and they are\npairwise distinct.  Their number equals the number of uncovered abscissae,\nwhich is \\(n-k\\).\n\nThus we obtain a collection of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny\nlines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  All lines are distinct,\nso the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The even\u2011\\(n\\) case\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, the construction above with \\(k=n\\) leaves uncovered\nexactly the points with \\(x+y=n+2\\); there are \\(n/2\\) of them, and no\nvertical line is available to cover them (because we would need \\(0\\)\nvertical lines).  Hence the construction fails for \\(k=n\\).\n\nFor any \\(k\\le n-1\\) the construction works, because at least one vertical\nline is present and it can be chosen to be \\(x=n+1\\), which covers all\nstill uncovered points with \\(x+y=n+2\\).  Therefore for even \\(n\\) all\nvalues \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\) are attainable, while \\(k=n\\) is impossible\nby the argument of Section\u202f2.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the existence part (Section\u202f4 and the even\u2011\\(n\\) refinement) with\nthe impossibility proved in Section\u202f2 yields the complete description of\nthe admissible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
