d\n\\Delta b_i=b_{i+1}-b_i<0 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nSince both \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_{i+1},b_{i+1})\\) satisfy (7),\n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\Delta a_i+\\beta\\Delta b_i=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{\\Delta b_i}{\\Delta a_i}=-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are non‑zero integers of opposite\nsign, whence  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the sum \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\).  From (9),\n\n\\[\ns_{i+1}-s_i=\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i .\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nBecause the signs of \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are opposite,\n\\(|\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i|\\le |\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|-2\\).  Using (11) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|s_{i+1}-s_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nHence the values \\(s_i\\) taken on the line differ by at least \\(2\\).\nFor points of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(2\\le s_i\\le n+1\\); the interval\n\\([2,n+1]\\) contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\nintegers that are pairwise at distance \\(\\ge2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points of\n\\(S_n\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume that all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.  \nBy Lemma 1 each line contributes at most \\(\\lfloor (n+1)/2\\rfloor\\) points,\nso the total number of covered points does not exceed  \n\n\\[\nn\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=\n\\begin{cases}\nn\\cdot\\frac{n+1}{2}= \\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}, & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\nn\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\dfrac{n^{2}}{2},   & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, \\(\\dfrac{n^{2}}{2}<\\dfrac{n(n+1)}{2}=|S_n|\\) by (6);\nhence the whole set \\(S_n\\) cannot be covered.  Therefore for even\n\\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A family of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1) .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\); thus \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\nA point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) iff \\(b=a+i-1\\); the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes \\(2a\\le n+2-i\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap S_n=\\bigl\\{(a,\\;a+i-1)\\mid\n1\\le a\\le\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\bigr\\},\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nand \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Step 1 – sunny lines.*  \nTake the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Step 2 – points still uncovered.*  \nRemove from \\(S_n\\) all points belonging to the chosen sunny lines.\nFor a fixed abscissa \\(x=a\\) the possible ordinates are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\).  \nAmong these, at most one equals \\(a+i-1\\) for some \\(i\\le k\\); therefore\nat most one point with abscissa \\(a\\) has already been covered.\nConsequently each uncovered abscissa appears **exactly once** among the\nremaining points.\n\n*Step 3 – vertical lines.*  \nFor every uncovered abscissa \\(a\\) draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\).\nThese lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they are\npairwise distinct.  Their number equals the number of uncovered abscissae,\nwhich is \\(n-k\\).\n\nThus we obtain a collection of \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\nlines that together contain every point of \\(S_n\\).  All lines are distinct,\nso the required configuration exists for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  The even‑\\(n\\) case\n\nWhen \\(n\\) is even, the construction above with \\(k=n\\) leaves uncovered\nexactly the points with \\(x+