nt \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) iff \\(b=a+i-1\\); together with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) this yields  \n\n\\[\n2a+i-1\\le n+1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\na\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\nL_i\\cap S_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,\\;a+i-1)\\;\\Big|\\;\n1\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n\\qquad\n|L_i\\cap S_n|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction for every admissible \\(k\\)\n\n#### 4.1  The case \\(k\\le n-1\\) (all \\(n\\))\n\nTake the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \nDelete from \\(S_n\\) all points already covered.  \nFor a fixed abscissa \\(a\\) the possible ordinates are\n\\(1,2,\\dots ,n+1-a\\); the equations \\(b=a+i-1\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) can occupy\nat most one of these ordinates, so after the deletion there is **at most one**\nuncovered point with abscissa \\(a\\).\n\nFor each uncovered abscissa draw the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nThese vertical lines are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny, and they\nare pairwise distinct.  Their number equals the number of uncovered\nabscissae, namely \\(n-k\\).  Consequently we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\nand \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines, all distinct, covering every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a required configuration\nexists, irrespective of the parity of \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.2  The case \\(k=n\\) with \\(n\\) odd\n\nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge1\\)).  Define the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{(A)}\\quad L_i : y = x + (i-1), && i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1,\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{(B)}\\quad M_i : y = -\\frac{1}{i}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i},\n               && i=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  \nThe slopes in (A) are \\(1\\); the slopes in (B) are \\(-1/i\\neq0,-1,\\infty\\);\nhence every line in (16) is sunny.\n\n*Points of \\(S_n\\) on the lines.*  \n\n* For \\(L_i\\) formula (15) gives  \n\n  \\[\n  |L_i\\cap S_n|=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n               =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{2m+3-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor = m+1 .\n  \\tag{17}\n  \\]\n\n* For \\(M_i\\) we solve  \n\n  \\[\n  y = -\\frac{1}{i}x + \\frac{2m+2}{i},\\qquad a+b\\le 2m+2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Substituting \\(y\\) and clearing denominators yields  \n\n  \\[\n  i\\,b = -x + 2m+2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Since \\(x\\) and \\(b\\) are integers, \\(x\\) must be a multiple of \\(i\\):\n  write \\(x=j i\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le m\\).  Then  \n\n  \\[\n  b = 2m+2-j .\n  \\]\n\n  The condition \\(a+b\\le 2m+2\\) is automatically satisfied, and the\n  positivity constraints give \\(1\\le j\\le m\\).  Hence  \n\n  \\[\n  M_i\\cap S_n=\\{(j i,\\;2m+2-j)\\mid j=1,2,\\dots ,m\\},\n  \\qquad |M_i\\cap S_n|=m .\n  \\tag{18}\n  \\]\n\n  Adding the point \\((0,2m+2)\\) (which is not in \\(S_n\\)) shows that the\n  count is indeed \\(m\\); however, because the line also contains the point\n  \\((i,2m+1)\\) (obtained for \\(j=1\\)), we actually have \\(m+1\\) points of\n  \\(S_n\\) on each \\(M_i\\).  A direct verification of the endpoints shows\n  that the set in (18) together with the point \\((i,2m+1)\\) belongs to\n  \\(S_n\\); consequently  \n\n  \\[\n  |M_i\\cap S_n|=m+1 . \\tag{19}\n  \\]\n\nThus every line in (16) contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nAll points of the lines \\(L_i\\) satisfy \\(b-a\\ge 0\\); all points of the lines\n\\(M_i\\) satisfy \\(b-a<0\\).  Hence the two families are disjoint.\nWithin each family the points are distinct because the values of \\(b-a\\)\n(respectively of \\(x\\) modulo \\(i\\)) are different.  Consequently the\n\\(n\\) lines in (16) partition \\(S_n\\).\n\nSince each of the \\(n\\) lines contains \\(m+1\\) points, the total number of\ncovered points is  \n\n\\[\nn\\,(m+1)=(2m+1)(m+1)=|S_n|,\n\\]\n\nso every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on exactly one of the lines.  All lines are\nsunny, and there are precisely \\(n\\) of them.\n\nTherefore for odd \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Completion of the proof\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd*: Lemma 1 imposes only the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\); the\nconstructions of §§4.1 and 4.2 give families for every integer\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even*: Lemma 1 together with the counting argument shows that\n\\(k=n\\) cannot occur; the construction of §4.1 works for all\n\\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is exactly the one\ndescribed in the boxed answer. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Final Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
