"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \n\nFor a given integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\[2mm]\n&\\text{if }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n   \\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n2. **Upper bound for a sunny line (Lemma\u202f1).**  \n   Any line that is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\) contains at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n3. **Consequences of Lemma\u202f1.**  \n   *If \\(n\\) is even*, the total number of points that can be covered by \\(n\\) sunny lines is at most  \n\n   \\[\n   n\\cdot\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|,\n   \\]\n\n   so \\(k=n\\) is impossible when \\(n\\) is even.  \n   *If \\(n\\) is odd*, (1) gives the weaker inequality \\(k\\le n\\); no further restriction follows from Lemma\u202f1.\n\n4. **A family of sunny lines.**  \n   For each integer \\(i\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;y=x+(i-1) .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \n   The points of \\(S_n\\) lying on \\(L_i\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap S_n=\\Bigl\\{(a,\\;a+i-1)\\;\\Big|\\;\n   1\\le a\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   i.e. \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+2-i}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n5. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(k\\le n-1\\) (all \\(n\\)).*  \n   Take the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).  \n   Remove from \\(S_n\\) all points already covered.  \n   For each remaining abscissa \\(a\\) there is at most one uncovered point with that abscissa, because the equations \\(y=a+i-1\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) already occupy at most one ordinate for the given \\(a\\).  \n   Draw, for every uncovered abscissa, the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n   These vertical lines are non\u2011sunny, are pairwise distinct, and their number equals the number of uncovered abscissae, namely \\(n-k\\).  \n   Consequently we obtain a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non\u2011sunny lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Case 2 \u2013 \\(k=n\\) and \\(n\\) odd.*  \n   Write \\(n=2m+1\\) (\\(m\\ge 1\\)).  \n   Define the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\text{(i)}\\quad  L_i : y = x+(i-1), && i=1,2,\\dots ,m+1,\\\\[2mm]\n   &\\text{(ii)}\\quad M_i : y = -\\frac{1}{i}\\,x + \\frac{n+1}{i}, && i=1,2,\\dots ,m .\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Why are they sunny?*  \n   The slopes in (i) are \\(1\\); the slopes in (ii) are \\(-1/i\\neq 0,-1,\\infty\\). Hence every line in (4) is sunny.\n\n   *How many points of \\(S_n\\) does each line contain?*  \n   For \\(L_i\\) formula (3) gives \\(|L_i\\cap S_n|=m+1\\).  \n   For \\(M_i\\) we solve the system\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{1}{i}x + \\frac{n+1}{i},\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\n   \\]\n   obtaining the integer solutions\n   \\[\n   (a,b)=\\bigl(j,\\;n+1-j-\\tfrac{j}{i}\\bigr),\\qquad\n   j= i,\\,2i,\\,\\dots ,\\,i m .\n   \\]\n   Hence each \\(M_i\\) also contains exactly \\(m+1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Disjointness.*  \n   The sets \\(\\{L_i\\cap S_n\\}_{i=1}^{m+1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they have distinct values of \\(b-a\\).  \n   The sets \\(\\{M_i\\cap S_n\\}_{i=1}^{m}\\) are pairwise disjoint because they have distinct values of \\(a\\) modulo \\(i\\).  \n   Moreover a point belonging to some \\(L_i\\) cannot belong to any \\(M_j\\) (the former satisfy \\(b-a\\ge 0\\) while the latter satisfy \\(b-a<0\\)).  \n   Consequently the \\(n\\) lines in (4) partition \\(S_n\\); their total number of covered points is\n   \\[\n   (m+1)(m+1)+m(m+1)= (2m+1)(m+1)=|S_n|.\n   \\]\n\n   Thus for odd \\(n\\) we have an explicit family of \\(n\\) sunny lines covering all points.\n\n6. **Putting everything together.**  \n   *If \\(n\\) is odd*, Lemma\u202f1 gives the trivial bound \\(k\\le n\\); the construction of \u00a75 provides families for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \n   *If \\(n\\) is even*, Lemma\u202f1 together with the counting argument shows that \\(k=n\\) is impossible; the construction of \u00a75 works for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\nHence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) describes a right\u2011isosceles triangle with vertices\n\\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\); a simple count gives  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma\u202f1 (bound for a sunny line)\n\nA line is **sunny** iff it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line\n\\(x+y=0\\).  \nWrite a sunny line in the integer form  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha x+\\beta y=\\gamma ,\\qquad \\alpha,\\beta\\in\\mathbb Z,\\;\n\\alpha\\beta\\neq0,\\;\\alpha+\\beta\\neq0,\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwith \\(\\gcd(\\alpha,\\beta)=1\\).\n\nLet \\((a_1,b_1),\\dots ,(a_t,b_t)\\) be the points of \\(S_n\\) that lie on the line,\nordered so that \\(a_1<a_2<\\dots<a_t\\).  \nBecause \\(\\beta\\neq0\\) the line is not vertical, hence the \\(b_i\\)\u2019s are strictly\ndecreasing:\n\n\\[\nb_1>b_2>\\dots>b_t . \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nFor consecutive points set  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta a_i=a_{i+1}-a_i>0,\\qquad\n\\Delta b_i=b_{i+1}-b_i<0 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBoth \\((a_i,b_i)\\) and \\((a_{i+1},b_{i+1})\\) satisfy (6), therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\alpha\\Delta a_i+\\beta\\Delta b_i=0\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{\\Delta b_i}{\\Delta a_i}=-\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are non\u2011zero integers of opposite sign,\nhence  \n\n\\[\n|\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nConsider the sums \\(s_i=a_i+b_i\\).  From (8),\n\n\\[\ns_{i+1}-s_i=\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause the signs of \\(\\Delta a_i\\) and \\(\\Delta b_i\\) are opposite,\n\\(|\\Delta a_i+\\Delta b_i|\\le |\\Delta a_i|+|\\Delta b_i|-2\\); using (10) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n|s_{i+1}-s_i|\\ge 2 . \\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nHence the values of \\(a+b\\) taken on the line differ by at least \\(2\\).\nFor points of \\(S_n\\) we have \\(2\\le a+b\\le n+1\\); the interval \\([2,n+1]\\)\ncontains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) integers that are pairwise\nat distance \\(\\ge2\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nt\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line contains at most \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\)\npoints of \\(S_n\\). \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Immediate consequences\n\n*If \\(n\\) is even.*  \nThen \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=\\frac n2\\).  \nIf all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, the total number of points they could cover\nwould be at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot\\frac n2=\\frac{n^{2}}2<\\frac{n(n+1)}2=|S_n|\n\\]\n\nby (5).  Hence for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is impossible.\n\n*If \\(n\\) is odd.*  \nWrite \\(n=2m+1\\).  Then \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor=m+1\\) and (13)\ngives \\(k\\le n\\); no further restriction follows from Lemma\u202f1.\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  A convenient family of sunny lines\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\ge1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + (i-1). \\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\