the lines.*  \nWe have used  \n\n\\[\nk\\ \\text{sunny lines }L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nn-k\\ \\text{vertical lines }V_{k+1},\\dots ,V_n .\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are distinct, and their total number is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\).  \nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of them.  \nHence for every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Non‑existence of a configuration with \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there are \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_n\\) covering \\(T_n\\) and that **all** of them are sunny.\n\n*Intersection with the left side.*  \nSince a sunny line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, each \\(\\ell_i\\) meets the vertical line \\(x=1\\) in exactly one point.  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) lying on \\(x=1\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,(1,n),\n\\]\n\nexactly \\(n\\) points.  \nBecause the \\(n\\) lines must cover all points of \\(T_n\\), each of them must contain a distinct point of this list; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_i\\cap\\{x=1\\}\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n   =\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Intersection with the right side.*  \nInside the triangle the only lattice point on the line \\(x=n\\) is \\((n,1)\\) (the condition \\(n+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\)).  \nA sunny line is not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, so each \\(\\ell_i\\) also meets \\(x=n\\) at exactly one point, which must be \\((n,1)\\).  \n\n*Consequences.*  \nTherefore each \\(\\ell_i\\) is the line through \\((1,b_i)\\) and \\((n,1)\\) for a unique \\(b_i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nThe slope of this line is  \n\n\\[\nm_i=\\frac{1-b_i}{\\,n-1\\,}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(b_i=1\\) then \\(m_i=0\\) (the line is horizontal, parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).  \nIf \\(b_i=n\\) then \\(m_i=-1\\) (the line is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).  \nBoth cases are forbidden for a sunny line.  \nHence at most the \\(n-2\\) lines corresponding to \\(b_i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) can be sunny.  \nSince we assumed that **all** \\(n\\) lines are sunny, we obtain a contradiction.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion\n\nThe construction of Section 1 shows that every integer  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]\n\nis attainable, while Section 2 proves that \\(k=n\\) is impossible.  \nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
