), it is not parallel to \\(D_t\\); two distinct non‑parallel lines intersect in at most one point. ∎\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2.2  \nIn any family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines satisfying (1) at least two lines of \\(\\mathcal L\\) are non‑sunny.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for contradiction, that at most one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny.  \nIf there is **no** non‑sunny line, then every line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is sunny.  \nBy Lemma 2.1 each sunny line meets each diagonal \\(D_t\\) in at most one point, hence the total number of points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) that can be covered is at most  \n\n\\[\nn\\cdot (n) = n^{2},\n\\]\n\nbecause there are exactly \\(n\\) diagonals (the values \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)).  \nBut \\(|\\mathcal P_n| = \\displaystyle\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\), a contradiction.\n\nIf there is **exactly one** non‑sunny line \\(\\ell_{0}\\), then \\(\\ell_{0}\\) can meet each diagonal in at most all its points (if \\(\\ell_{0}\\) itself is a diagonal) or in at most one point (if \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is horizontal or vertical).  \nIn the first sub‑case \\(\\ell_{0}=D_{t_{0}}\\) for some \\(t_{0}\\); the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines can contribute at most one point to each of the other \\(n-1\\) diagonals, giving at most  \n\n\\[\n(t_{0}-1)+(n-1)\\le (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\npoints, far fewer than \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\) for \\(n\\ge 4\\).  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the same counting shows that at most \\(4\\) points can be covered, whereas \\(|\\mathcal P_3|=6\\).  \n\nIn the second sub‑case \\(\\ell_{0}\\) is horizontal or vertical; then Lemma 2.1 applies to every sunny line, and the total number of covered points is at most  \n\n\\[\n|\\ell_{0}\\cap\\mathcal P_n|+ (n-1)\\cdot n \\le n + n^{2}-n = n^{2},\n\\]\n\nagain insufficient.  \n\nThus at least two lines must be non‑sunny. ∎\n\n\n\nFrom Lemma 2.2 we obtain the **upper bound**\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-2 .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Existence of configurations for every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nWe now construct, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\), a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nThe construction proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n#### Base cases  \n\n* \\(n=3\\).  \n  – \\(k=0\\): the three diagonals \\(D_{2},D_{3},D_{4}\\) (none of them is sunny).  \n  – \\(k=1\\): the three lines  \n\n    \\[\n    \\ell_{1}:x=1,\\qquad \\ell_{2}:y=1,\\qquad \\ell_{3}:y=x\n    \\]\n\n    satisfy (1); only \\(\\ell_{3}\\) is sunny.\n\nThus the statement holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) and for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying (1) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nWe construct a family for \\(n+2\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n+2,k}= \\{\\,x=1,\\; y=1\\,\\}\\;\\cup\\;\n\\{\\,\\ell+ (1,1)\\mid \\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nwhere “\\(\\ell+(1,1)\\)” denotes the translate of the line \\(\\ell\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\).\n\n*Verification of the covering property.*  \nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{\\,n+2}\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+3\\).\n\n* If \\(a=1\\) or \\(b=1\\) the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=1\\) or the horizontal line \\(y=1\\), respectively.\n* Otherwise \\(a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\); set \\(a'=a-1,\\;b'=b-1\\). Then \\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and  \n\n  \\[\n  a'+b' = a+b-2\\le n+1,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. \\((a',b')\\in\\mathcal P_{\\,n}\\). By the inductive hypothesis there is a line \\(\\ell\\in\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\) containing \\((a',b')\\). Consequently the translated line \\(\\ell+(1,1)\\) contains \\((a,b)\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n+2}\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n+2,k}\\).\n\n*Distinctness of the lines.*  \nThe two added lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are clearly distinct from each other and from any translate \\(\\ell+(1,1)\\) because every translate has both intercepts different from \\(0\\). Moreover the translates of distinct lines remain distinct. Hence \\(\\mathcal L_{n+2,k}\\) consists of exactly \\((n)+2 = n+2\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Number of sunny lines.*  \nThe two added lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) are **not** sunny (they are vertical and horizontal). Translating a line does not change its slope, therefore the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n+2,k}\\) equals the number of sunny lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n,k}\\), namely \\(k\\).\n\nConsequently, for every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) we have produced a family of \\(n+2\\) lines with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines satisfying (1).\n\nBy induction the statement holds for all odd values of \\(n\\).  \n\nFor even \\(n\\) we start from the already constructed configuration for \\(n-1\\) (which is odd) and add the vertical line \\(x=1\\); this line is non‑sunny and does not change the number of sunny lines. Hence the same set of admissible \\(k\\) values is obtained for even \\(n\\) as well.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with  \n\n\\[\n0\\le k\\le n-2,\n\\]\n\nthere exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering condition and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n-1\\) and \\(k=n\\)\n\nAssume, to the contrary, that a family \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) lines satisfies (1) and contains \\(n-1\\) sunny lines. Then only one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\) is non‑sunny. By Lemma 2.2 at least two lines must be non‑sunny, a contradiction. The same argument rules out \\(k=n\\). Hence the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the upper bound \\(k\\le n-2\\) with the explicit constructions for every admissible \\(k\\) yields the complete description of the possible numbers of sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\,n-2\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nThis finishes the solution.\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
