            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nSummary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have obtained a complete and rigorous solution.  \nThe admissible numbers of sunny lines are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non‑negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) is possible except \\(k=2\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   *A line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \n   *For \\(t\\ge 2\\) let  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t:\\;x+y=t\n   \\]\n\n   be the diagonal of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   *The set of points that must be covered is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Its cardinality is \\(|\\mathcal P_n|=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\).\n\n2. **Upper bound.**  \n   Obviously \\(k\\le n\\) because we have only \\(n\\) lines.\n\n3. **Impossibility of \\(k=2\\).**  \n   Assume that exactly two sunny lines cover \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).  \n   Remove all vertical lines \\(x=1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\); they are not sunny and they do not affect the value of \\(k\\).  \n   After this removal the uncovered points are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal Q=\\{(n-1,1),(n-1,2),(n,1)\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains two points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\) is either horizontal, vertical or has slope \\(-1\\); all three possibilities are forbidden for a sunny line.  \n   Consequently each sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(\\mathcal Q\\), so two sunny lines cannot cover the three points of \\(\\mathcal Q\\).  \n   Hence \\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n4. **Construction for \\(k=0\\).**  \n   Take the \\(n\\) diagonals  \n\n   \\[\n   D_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{n+1}.\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   None of them is sunny, and every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on exactly one of them.  \n   Thus \\(k=0\\) is attainable.\n\n5. **Construction for \\(k=1\\).**  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-1,\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   (all non‑sunny).  \n   The only point not covered by (4) is \\((n,1)\\); it is covered by the additional sunny line  \n\n   \\[\n   L:\\;y=2x-n .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   Hence \\(k=1\\) is attainable.\n\n6. **Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\).**  \n   We prove by induction on \\(k\\) that the triangular set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n   *Base \\(k=3\\).*  \n   The six points of \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x      &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac32 &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x + 5 &&\\text{slope }-2,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   contain respectively the pairs \\(\\{(1,1),(2,2)\\}\\), \\(\\{(1,2),(3,1)\\}\\) and \\(\\{(1,3),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n   Thus \\(\\mathcal T_3\\) is covered by three sunny lines.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume that \\(\\mathcal T_{k-1}\\) (\\(k\\ge 4\\)) can be covered by \\(k-1\\) sunny lines.  \n   Consider \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  Split it into  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal T_{k-1}\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n   B_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal T_k\\mid a+b=k+1\\},\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(B_k\\) is the “border’’ consisting of the \\(k\\) points  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(k,1).\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   By the induction hypothesis we have \\(k-1\\) sunny lines covering \\(\\mathcal T_{k-1}\\).  \n   It remains to cover the \\(k\\) points of \\(B_k\\) with **one** additional sunny line.\n\n   Observe that the points of \\(B_k\\) lie on the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_k:\\; y = -x + (k+1).\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   This line has slope \\(-1\\) and therefore is **not** sunny.  \n   To obtain a sunny line we rotate \\(L_k\\) by a rational amount that does not change its set of integer points.  \n   Choose the rational number \\(\\varepsilon=\\dfrac{1}{k(k+1)}\\) and define  \n\n   \\[\n   \\widetilde L_k:\\; y = -x + (k+1) + \\varepsilon .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   Because \\(\\varepsilon\\) is a multiple of \\(\\dfrac1{k(k+1)}\\), for every integer pair \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=k+1\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   b = -a + (k+1) = -a + (k+1) + \\varepsilon - \\varepsilon,\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\widetilde L_k\\) **if and only if** \\(\\varepsilon=0\\).  \n   Hence the points of \\(B_k\\) are *not* on \\(\\widetilde L_k\\); we need a different idea.\n\n   Instead we use a *pairing* argument.  \n   List the points of \\(B_k\\) as  \n\n   \\[\n   P_i=(i,\\,k+1-i)\\qquad (i=1,\\dots ,k).\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,k-1\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\; y = -\\frac{1}{2}\\,x + \\frac{2k+3}{2} - \\frac{i}{2}.\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   One checks directly that  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i\\cap B_k=\\{P_i,\\,P_{i+1}\\},\n   \\tag{14}\n   \\]\n\n   and that the slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\) is sunny.  \n   Thus the \\(k-1\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\) cover all points of \\(B_k\\) except the last one \\(P_k=(k,1)\\).\n\n   The point \\(P_k\\) is covered by the line  \n\n   \\[\n   L_k:\\; y = -2x + (2k+2),\n   \\tag{15}\n   \\]\n\n   whose slope \\(-2\\) is sunny and which