 L_k\\) covering \\(\\mathcal T_k\\).  \n   Translating them back by adding \\(n-k\\) to the \\(x\\)-coordinate yields \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_i:\\;(x,y)\\in\\widetilde L_i\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\ (x-(n-k),y)\\in\\widetilde L_i,\n   \\qquad i=1,\\dots ,k,\n   \\tag{20}\n   \\]\n\n   which together with the vertical lines (18) cover every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n   The construction uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   *Impossibility*: \\(k=2\\) cannot occur (step 3).  \n   *Possibility*: \\(k=0\\) (step 4), \\(k=1\\) (step 5) and every \\(k\\ge 3\\) with \\(k\\le n\\) (steps 6–7).  \n\n   Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n   \\]\n\n   for every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
