\(n\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   k=k'=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n        =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n5. **All smaller \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from a configuration with the maximal number of sunny lines, replace any sunny line by a suitable non‑sunny line (horizontal, vertical or diagonal) while keeping the covering property.  \n   Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le\\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) is realizable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |\\mathcal{P}_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Three lines that must be non‑sunny\n\nConsider the three subsets of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\)\n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(a,1)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n\\},\\qquad\nD=\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly \\(n\\) points; the three intersections are\n\\((1,1),(1,n),(n,1)\\).  \nHence the total number of distinct points in \\(C\\cup R\\cup D\\) is \\(3n-3\\).\n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three families\n\\(\\{x=1\\},\\{y=1\\},\\{x+y=n+1\\}\\); consequently it meets each of the\nsets \\(C,R,D\\) in **at most one point**.  \n\nIf, for instance, the vertical line \\(x=1\\) were absent, then every point\nof \\(C\\) would have to be covered by a sunny line, which would require at\nleast \\(n\\) sunny lines.  Since we have only \\(n\\) lines in total, this is\nimpossible unless all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, a case that will be ruled\nout later.  Therefore the line \\(x=1\\) must belong to the family of\nnon‑sunny lines.  The same argument forces the presence of the horizontal\nline \\(y=1\\) and the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).\n\nThus **any admissible configuration contains the three distinct\nnon‑sunny lines**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,x=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=n+1\\,}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Reduction to a smaller instance\n\nRemove the three lines (1) from the configuration.  \nThe points that remain uncovered are precisely\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{Q}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2\\}\n            =\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a'=a-1,\\;b'=b-1\\); then \\((a',b')\\) satisfies\n\\(a',b'\\ge 1\\) and \\(a'+b'\\le (n-2)+1\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal{Q}_n\\) is a\ntranslated copy of \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-2}\\).\n\nAfter deleting the three lines (1) we have \\(n-3\\) lines left, of which at\nmost the original number \\(k\\) are sunny (the three removed lines are\nnon‑sunny).  Consequently the sub‑problem “cover \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-2}\\) with\n\\(n-2\\) lines” is now asked with **one line fewer**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Inductive upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\max\\{\\,k\\mid\\text{there exist }n\\text{ distinct lines covering }\n\\mathcal{P}_n\\text{ with exactly }k\\text{ sunny lines}\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nFrom the discussion above we obtain, for every \\(n\\ge5\\),\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 1+f(n-2). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIndeed, the three forced non‑sunny lines consume three of the \\(n\\) lines,\nleaving \\(n-3\\) lines for the reduced instance \\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-2}\\); the\nmaximum number of sunny lines that can appear among those \\(n-3\\) lines is\nat most \\(f(n-2)\\), and we have not used any sunny line among the three\nmandatory ones, whence (2).\n\nThe initial values are obtained by direct inspection:\n\n* \\(n=3\\): the six points can be covered with three lines; at most one of\n  them can be sunny, so \\(f(3)=1\\).\n* \\(n=4\\): a covering with three non‑sunny lines exists, and a covering\n  with exactly one sunny line exists, but two sunny lines are impossible\n  (see the construction below). Hence \\(f(4)=1\\).\n\nApplying (2) repeatedly yields\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor ,\\qquad n\\ge3. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4. Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe prove by induction that the bound (3) is sharp.\n\n*Base cases.*  \nFor \\(n=3\\) the three lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=4\n\\]\n\ncover all points; adding the sunny line \\(x=y\\) gives a configuration with\n\\(k=1=\\big\\lfloor\\frac{3-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\).  \nFor \\(n=4\\) the four lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;y=1,\\;x+y=5,\\;x=y\n\\]\n\ncover \\(\\mathcal{P}_4\\) and contain exactly one sunny line,\nagain attaining \\(\\big\\lfloor\\frac{4-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor=1\\).\n\n*Inductive step.*  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n-2}\\) for the set\n\\(\\mathcal{P}_{\\,n-2}\\) with  \n\n\\[\nk'=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsunny lines exists.  Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_{n-2}\\) by the\nvector \\((1,1)\\); the slopes are unchanged, so the translated lines are\nstill sunny.  Add the three mandatory non‑sunny lines (1).  The resulting\nfamily \\(\\mathcal{L}_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, covers all\npoints of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) (the translation makes the lines cover exactly\nthe interior points \\(\\mathcal{Q}_n\\)), and contains\n\n\\[\nk=k'=\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-3}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n   =\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\n\\]\n\nsunny lines.  Thus the bound is attained for \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5. Obtaining all smaller values of \\(k\\)\n\nStarting from a configuration with the maximal number\n\\(k_{\\max}= \\big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\big\\rfloor\\) of sunny lines, we may\nreplace any sunny line by a suitable non‑sunny line (for instance, by a\nhorizontal line passing through one of its points).  Such a replacement\ndoes not destroy the covering property because the point that was covered\nby the sunny line remains covered by the new non‑sunny line, and all other\npoints stay covered by the remaining lines.  Repeating this operation we\ncan reduce the number of sunny lines to any integer between \\(0\\) and\n\\(k_{\\max}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6. Conclusion\n\nThe admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the integers\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,0\\le k\\le\\Big\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Big\\rfloor\\,}.\n\\]\n\nBoth the upper bound and the constructive realisation have been proved\nrigorously. ∎"
