e must have at least two non\u2011sunny lines, i.e.  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-2 .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(n=4\\) the same inequality gives \\(k\\le2\\); for \\(n=3\\) it gives \\(k\\le2\\) as well.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   *Base cases.*  \n   - \\(n=3\\): the three lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_1:x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad L_3:\\;y=2x+1\n     \\]\n\n     are distinct, the first one is non\u2011sunny, the other two are sunny, and they cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  Hence \\(k=2\\) is attainable.  \n   - \\(n=4\\): the four lines  \n\n     \\[\n     L_1:y=1,\\qquad L_2:x+y=3,\\qquad L_3:y=x,\\qquad L_4:y=2x+1\n     \\]\n\n     (the first two are non\u2011sunny, the last two sunny) cover \\(\\mathcal P_4\\); thus \\(k=2=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  \n   Assume for some \\(n\\ge5\\) that a configuration \\(\\mathcal C_{n-2}\\) of \\(n-2\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_{\\,n-2}\\) exists with exactly \\((n-2)-2=n-4\\) sunny lines (i.e. with the maximal possible number \\(k_{\\max}=n-4\\)).  \n\n   Translate every line of \\(\\mathcal C_{n-2}\\) by the vector \\((1,1)\\); the translation does not change slopes, so sunny lines stay sunny.  The translated family covers the set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(a,b)\\mid a,b\\ge2,\\;a+b\\le n\\}= \\mathcal P_n\\setminus\\bigl(\\{x=1\\}\\cup\\{y=1\\}\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n   Adding the two non\u2011sunny lines \\(x=1\\) and \\(y=1\\) we obtain a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) with  \n\n   \\[\n   k = (n-4) = n-2\n   \\]\n\n   sunny lines.  Thus the bound (1) is sharp for every \\(n\\ge4\\).\n\n4. **Realising all smaller values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from a configuration with the maximal number of sunny lines, replace any sunny line by a suitable non\u2011sunny line (for instance, by a vertical line through one of its points).  This operation reduces \\(k\\) by one while preserving the covering property.  Repeating the operation yields configurations for every integer \\(k\\) between \\(0\\) and the maximal value found in step\u202f3.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \nIf a line contains three (or more) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) then it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSuppose a line \\(L\\) contains three distinct points \\((a_1,b_1),(a_2,b_2),(a_3,b_3)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\nBecause the points are lattice points, the slope of \\(L\\) is a rational number\n\\(\\frac{p}{q}\\) with coprime integers \\(p,q\\).  If \\(|p|,|q|\\ge2\\) then the\nhorizontal and vertical distances between consecutive lattice points on \\(L\\) are at\nleast \\(2\\), which forces the sum \\(a+b\\) to increase by at least \\(2\\) when we\nmove from one lattice point of \\(L\\) to the next.  Consequently at most two\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) can lie on such a line, because the condition\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) would be violated after the second step.  Hence a line with\nthree lattice points must have \\(|p|=0\\) (horizontal), \\(|q|=0\\) (vertical) or\n\\(|p|=|q|\\) (slope \\(-1\\)).  \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 2  \nEvery sunny line meets \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) in at most two points.\n\n*Proof.*  \nA sunny line is, by definition, not parallel to any of the three families\nmentioned in Lemma\u202f1; therefore Lemma\u202f1 applies and yields the desired bound. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nLet a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfy the covering condition and\nlet \\(k\\) be the number of sunny lines among them.  Denote by \\(t=n-k\\) the\nnumber of non\u2011sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(t\\ge2\\)* then \\(k\\le n-2\\) immediately.\n\n*Assume \\(t=1\\).*  \nLet the unique non\u2011sunny line be \\(L\\).  At most \\(n\\) points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\)\nlie on \\(L\\) (the intersection of \\(L\\) with the triangle \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is a\nsegment of length at most \\(n\\)).  Hence at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal P_n|-n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2-n=\\frac{n(n-1)}2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\npoints are not on \\(L\\).  By Lemma\u202f2 each sunny line can cover at most two of\nthose points, so with \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines we could cover at most \\(2(n-1)\\)\npoints.  Necessarily  \n\n\\[\n2(n-1)\\ge\\frac{n(n-1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nIf \\(n\\ge5\\) the left\u2011hand side is smaller than the right\u2011hand side, a\ncontradiction.  Therefore a configuration with only one non\u2011sunny line can exist\nonly for \\(n\\le4\\).  For \\(n=4\\) the inequality is an equality, and for\n\\(n=3\\) it is also satisfied.\n\nConsequently\n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\n2, & n=3,\\\\[1mm]\n2, & n=4,\\\\[1mm]\nn-2, & n\\ge5 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining the bound\n\nWe now show that the bounds in (3) are sharp.\n\n*Case \\(n=3\\).*  \nThe three lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;y=2x-1,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=2x+1\n\\]\n\nare distinct; \\(L_1\\) is non\u2011sunny, \\(L_2,L_3\\) are sunny.  They cover the six\npoints of \\(\\mathcal P_3\\).  Hence \\(k=2\\) is attainable.\n\n*Case \\(n=4\\).*  \nThe four lines  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;y=1,\\qquad\nL_2:\\;x+y=3,\\qquad\nL_3:\\;y=x,\\qquad\nL_4:\\;y=2x+1\n\\]\n\ncover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_4\\); \\(L_1,L_2\\) are non\u2011sunny, \\(L_3,L_4\\) are\nsunny.  Thus \\(k=2=n-2\\) is attainable.\n\n*Inductive step for \\(n\\ge5\\).*  \nAssume a configuration \\(\\