hem is taken by a distinct\n   sunny line of slope \\(2\\) (for instance the line through \\((i,k+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,k-i)\\)).\n   Thus we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering all of \\(T_k\\).*\n\n   In both parity cases the construction uses precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines,\n   all distinct and none parallel to the forbidden directions.\n\n   Consequently, for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (2) we have produced\n   a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   that covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The necessary condition (2) is also sufficient, giving the complete\n   description of admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad |S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its direction vector is not a scalar multiple of\n\\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nA sunny line cannot be horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\).\nAmong all sunny directions the one that can contain the most lattice\npoints of the triangle \\(S_n\\) is the direction \\((1,1)\\) (slope \\(1\\)).\nIndeed, a line of slope \\(1\\) consists of the points with a fixed\ndifference \\(a-b=d\\).  For a given \\(d\\),\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a-b=d\\}\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nThe maximum of this expression is attained at \\(d=0\\) and equals  \n\n\\[\nm_n:=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nHence **any sunny line contains at most \\(m_n\\) points of \\(S_n\\).**\n\nConsider the family of diagonal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D=\\{\\,x+y=c\\mid c=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\,\\}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal D\\) are non‑sunny; the line with parameter \\(c\\)\ncontains exactly \\(c-1\\) points of \\(S_n\\).  The lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\)\npartition \\(S_n\\).\n\nSuppose we are given a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines that covers\n\\(S_n\\) and contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\nDiscard all sunny lines and keep only the \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nSince the non‑sunny lines can be chosen among the family \\(\\mathcal D\\),\nwe may assume (by possibly rotating the whole picture) that they are\nthe \\(n-k\\) lines of \\(\\mathcal D\\) with the largest parameters,\ni.e. the lines \\(x+y=c\\) for  \n\n\\[\nc=n+1,n,\\dots ,n-k+2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines cover  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{c=n-k+2}^{n+1}(c-1)\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\npoints, leaving exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\npoints uncovered.  All these remaining points must lie on the \\(k\\) sunny\nlines.  Because each sunny line can contain at most \\(m_n\\) points,\n(5) yields the inequality  \n\n\\[\nk\\,m_n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nDividing by \\(k>0\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nm_n\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2},\n\\qquad\\text{hence}\\qquad\nk\\le 2m_n-1 .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nUsing (5),\n\n\\[\n2m_n-1=\n\\begin{cases}\n2\\bigl(\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr)-1=n, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[2mm]\n2\\bigl(\\frac{n}{2}\\bigr)-1=n-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\\nn-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n\\end{cases}}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction attaining every integer \\(k\\) that satisfies (12)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and satisfying (12).\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part\n\nTake the \\(n-k\\) diagonal lines (7).  They are non‑sunny and together\ncover all points of \\(S_n\\) except those with  \n\n\\[\na+b\\le k+1 .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nDenote this uncovered set by  \n\n\\[\nT_k=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\qquad |T_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.2  Covering \\(T_k\\) with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nBecause of (12) we have  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\le k\\,m_n,\n\\qquad m_n=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nHence the total “capacity’’ of \\(k\\) sunny lines (each can hold at most\n\\(m_n\\) points) is at least \\(|T_k|\\); we now exhibit an explicit\npartition of \\(T_k\\) into \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n**Case 1 – \\(k\\) odd.**  \nWrite \\(k=2r+1\\) (\\(r\\ge0\\)).  \nConsider the \\(k\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\; y=x+d,\\qquad d=-r,-r+1,\\dots ,r .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_d\\) meets \\(T_k\\) in exactly \\(r+1\\) points (the points with\n\\(a-b=d\\) and \\(a+b\\le 2r+2\\)).  Consequently the \\(k\\) lines (16) cover\n\n\\[\nk\\,(r+1)= (2r+1)(r+1)=\\frac{k(k+1)}2=|T_k|\n\\]\n\npoints, i.e. all of \\(T_k\\).  All these lines have slope \\(1\\), which is\nsunny.\n\n---\n\n**Case 2 – \\(k\\) even.**  \nWrite \\(k=2r\\) (\\(r\\ge1\\)).  \nFirst use the \\(2r-1\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:\\; y=x+d,\\qquad d=-r+1,\\dots ,r-1 .\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nEach of them contains exactly \\(r\\) points of \\(T_k\\); together they cover\n\n\\[\n(2r-1)r=\\frac{k(k-1)}2\n\\]\n\npoints.  The remaining \\(r\\) points of \\(T_k\\) are\n\n\\[\n(1,k),\\;(2,k-1),\\dots ,(r,k+1-r).\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i=1,\\dots ,r\\) draw the line of slope \\(2\\) passing through\n\\((i,k+1-i)\\) and \\((i+1,k-i)\\); explicitly\n\n\\[\n\\lambda_i:\\; y=2x+(k-2i+1).\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\lambda_i\\) contains exactly the single point \\((i,k+1-i)\\) from\n\\(T_k\\) and is sunny (slope \\(2\\)).  Adding the \\(r\\) lines (19) to the\n\\(2r-1\\) lines (17) yields precisely \\(k=2r\\) sunny lines covering all\npoints of \\(T_k\\).\n\n---\n\nThus for every admissible \\(k\\) we have produced:\n\n* \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines (7), and  \n* \\(k\\) sunny lines as described above,\n\nwhich together are \\(n\\) distinct lines covering the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe inequality (12) shows that no configuration can have more sunny\nlines than stated.  The constructions above demonstrate that for every\n\\(k\\) satisfying (12) such a configuration exists.  Hence the set of\npossible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one described in (12).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]"
