ssing through the\n   centre of the triangle, e.g. \\(y=x\\).)\n\n3. **How many points can be covered by non‑sunny lines?**  \n   The three families of non‑sunny lines are  \n\n   * horizontal: \\(y=b\\;(b=1,\\dots ,n)\\);\n   * vertical:   \\(x=a\\;(a=1,\\dots ,n)\\);\n   * diagonal:   \\(x+y=c\\;(c=2,\\dots ,n+1)\\).\n\n   A line of the first two families contains exactly \\(n+1-b\\) (resp. \\(n+1-a\\)) points of\n   \\(S_n\\); a diagonal line \\(x+y=c\\) contains \\(c-1\\) points.\n   Diagonal lines are pairwise disjoint, while any horizontal or vertical line meets each\n   diagonal line in a distinct point.  \n\n   **Lemma 3.1**  \n   *For a given integer \\(t\\;(1\\le t\\le n)\\) the largest possible number of *distinct*\n   points of \\(S_n\\) that can be covered by \\(t\\) non‑sunny lines is obtained by taking the\n   \\(t\\) diagonal lines with the largest parameters, i.e. the lines*\n   \\[\n   x+y=c\\qquad c=n+1,\\;n,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-t+2 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   *These lines are disjoint and contain respectively \\(n,\\;n-1,\\dots ,n-t+1\\) points; hence the\n   maximal number of distinct points is*\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=n-t+1}^{n} i=\\frac{t\\bigl(2n-t+1\\bigr)}2 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Any non‑sunny line contributes at most its own number of points, and equality can hold\n   only when the chosen lines are pairwise disjoint.  Among the three families only the\n   diagonal family is pairwise disjoint; therefore any optimal collection can be transformed\n   into a collection of diagonal lines without decreasing the number of covered points.\n   Replacing a diagonal line by a horizontal (or vertical) line of the same size would create\n   overlaps and strictly reduce the number of *distinct* points.  Consequently the optimal\n   choice consists solely of diagonal lines, and to maximise the total we pick those with the\n   largest sizes, which are exactly the lines (3).  ∎\n\n4. **Necessary condition for \\(k\\).**  \n   Let a configuration contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines; then it contains \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny\n   lines.  By Lemma 3.1 the non‑sunny lines can cover at most  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{(n-k)\\bigl(2n-(n-k)+1\\bigr)}2\n   =\\frac{n(n+1)}2-\\frac{k(k+1)}2\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   distinct points of \\(S_n\\).  Hence at least \\(\\dfrac{k(k+1)}2\\) points must be covered by\n   the sunny lines.\n\n   Each sunny line contains at most \\(m_n\\) points (2); therefore\n\n   \\[\n   k\\,m_n\\;\\ge\\;\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n   \\]\n\n   For \\(k>0\\) we may divide by \\(k\\) and obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   m_n\\ge\\frac{k+1}{2}\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n   k\\le 2m_n-1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Using (2),\n\n   \\[\n   2m_n-1=\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   Thus any admissible configuration must satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\n   k\\le\n   \\begin{cases}\n   n,   & n\\text{ odd},\\\\\n   n-1, & n\\text{ even}.\n   \\end{cases}}\n   \\tag{8}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Covering the triangular set \\(S_t\\) with \\(t\\) sunny lines (odd \\(t\\)).**  \n\n   **Lemma 5.1**  \n   *If \\(t\\) is odd, the set \\(S_t\\) can be covered by exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(t=2r+1\\).  For each integer \\(d\\) with \\(-r\\le d\\le r\\) consider the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d:\\;y=x+d .\n   \\tag{9}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(1\\) is sunny.  For \\(|d|\\le r\\) the points of \\(S_t\\) satisfying\n   \\(a-b=d\\) are exactly those with \\(a+b\\le t+1\\) and \\(a-b=d\\); their number is\n   \\(r+1\\).  Consequently the \\(t\\) lines (9) together contain  \n\n   \\[\n   t(r+1)=\\frac{t(t+1)}2=|S_t|\n   \\]\n\n   points, i.e. all of \\(S_t\\). ∎\n\n6. **Covering the triangular set \\(S_t\\) with \\(t\\) sunny lines (even \\(t\\)).**  \n\n   **Lemma 5.2**  \n   *If \\(t\\) is even, the set \\(S_t\\) can be covered by exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.*\n\n   *Proof.*  \n   Write \\(t=2r\\) with \\(r\\ge1\\).  \n   First take the \\(r\\) lines of slope \\(1\\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\;y=x+(i-1)\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,r) .\n   \\tag{10}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains the points of \\(S_t\\) whose difference \\(a-b\\) equals \\(i-1\\);\n   for \\(i\\le r\\) this yields exactly \\(r\\) points, namely the points with\n   \\(a+b\\le t+1\\) and \\(a-b=i-1\\).  Hence the \\(r\\) lines (10) cover \\(r^2\\) points.\n\n   The remaining points of \\(S_t\\) are those with \\(a-b\\ge r\\); they form the set  \n\n   \\[\n   R=\\{(a,b)\\in S_t\\mid a-b\\ge r\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   For each integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le r\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\lambda_j:\\;y=2x+(t-2j+1) .\n   \\tag{11}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope \\(2\\) is sunny.  A direct substitution shows that \\(\\lambda_j\\) contains\n   precisely the single point \\((j,t+1-j)\\) of \\(R\\).  Thus the \\(r\\) lines (11) cover the\n   remaining \\(r\\) points of \\(S_t\\).  Altogether we have \\(r+r=t\\) sunny lines covering\n   all of \\(S_t\\). ∎\n\n   Lemma 5.2 together with Lemma 5.1 shows that **for every positive integer \\(t\\) the\n   triangular set \\(S_t\\) can be covered by exactly \\(t\\) sunny lines.**\n\n7. **Sufficiency of the bound (construction for the original problem).**  \n\n   Let \\(k\\) satisfy the inequality (8).  We construct \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of\n   which are sunny, covering all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n   *Non‑sunny part.*  \n   Set \\(t=n-k\\).  Choose the \\(t\\) diagonal lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=c\\qquad c=n+1,\\;n,\\;\\dots ,\\;k+2 .\n   \\tag{12}\n   \\]\n\n   They are non‑sunny, pairwise disjoint, and together cover every point of \\(S_n\\) whose\n   sum \\(a+b\\) is at least \\(k+2\\).  The uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_k=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n   \\tag{13}\n   \\]\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   By Lemma 5.1 (if \\(k\\) is odd) or Lemma 5.2 (if \\(k\\) is even) the set \\(S_k\\) can be\n   covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Adding these \\(k\\) lines to the \\(t\\) diagonal\n   lines (12) yields a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny,\n   and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n   The construction works for every \\(k\\) obeying (8); when \\(n\\) is even the case\n   \\(k=n\\) is excluded because then \\(T_k=S_n\\) would have to be covered by \\(n\\) sunny\n   lines, contradicting the capacity bound (2).\n\n8. **Conclusion.**  \n   The inequality (8) is necessary, and the construction above shows it is also sufficient.\n   Hence the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly those described in (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Answer**\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
